rail.prompt:
You are a code reviewer tasked with identifying "Data Class" technical debt in the provided code snippets.
Please review the code snippet and identify any instances of Data Class technical debt.

Below, you will find a definition of "Data Class" technical debt along with example code snippets.
Each example pair consists of a "Bad Example" that includes "Data Class" technical debt and a "Good Example" where the technical debt has been resolved.

######################################################
Technical Debt: Data Class - It is a class that primarily consists of fields (attributes) and lacks meaningful behavior (methods) beyond simple getters and setters. 
        It is often used to store and transfer data, making it useful in certain situations like data transfer objects or persistence models.
        However, in the context of object-oriented design, a Data Class becomes a code smell when it represents a domain object that should encapsulate both data and behavior. 
        Overusing Data Classes as domain objects undermines the principles of encapsulation and responsibility distribution, leading to an anemic domain model. 
        To resolve this smell, the class is enriched by moving relevant behaviors into it or using more cohesive design strategies to distribute responsibilities.

Bad Example 4.1: Data Class

class Product {
    private String name;
    private double price;

    public String getName() {
        return name;
    }

    public void setName(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }

    public double getPrice() {
        return price;
    }

    public void setPrice(double price) {
        this.price = price;
    }
}

    - Issue: The Product class is a typical Data Class that simply holds data without encapsulating any behavior. 
    If this class is part of a domain model, it should handle relevant operations, like applying discounts or managing stock, instead of being a passive data holder.

Good Example 4.2: No Data Class

class Product {
    private String name;
    private double price;
    private int stockQuantity;

    public Product(String name, double price, int stockQuantity) {
        this.name = name;
        this.price = price;
        this.stockQuantity = stockQuantity;
    }

    public void applyDiscount(double percentage) {
        if (percentage > 0 && percentage <= 100) {
            this.price -= price * (percentage / 100);
        }
    }

    public boolean isInStock() {
        return stockQuantity > 0;
    }

    public void reduceStock(int quantity) {
        if (quantity > 0 && quantity <= stockQuantity) {
            stockQuantity -= quantity;
        }
    }

    public String getName() {
        return name;
    }

    public double getPrice() {
        return price;
    }

    public int getStockQuantity() {
        return stockQuantity;
    }
}

    - Benefit: The Product class has been refactored to include methods like applyDiscount, isInStock, and reduceStock. 
    This change transforms the class from a passive data holder into an active participant in the business logic, adhering to object-oriented design principles.

######################################################
Please analyze the following code snippets and report any data classes present in the code snippet.

######################################################
